# Next.js App Router: Best Practices in 2025

Next.js 14 and beyond bring powerful improvements to the App Router. Let's explore the best practices.

## Server Components by Default

All components are server components by default:

```typescript
// app/dashboard/page.tsx
export default function DashboardPage() {
  // This runs on the server
  const data = await fetchDashboardData();
  return <div>{data.title}</div>;
}
```

## Client Components When Needed

Use the 'use client' directive for interactivity:

```typescript
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```

## Data Fetching Patterns

### Server-Side Data Fetching

Fetch directly in server components:

```typescript
export default async function Blog({ params }) {
  const post = await fetch(
    `https://api.example.com/posts/${params.slug}`
  ).then(res => res.json());

  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </article>
  );
}
```

## Error Handling

Create error boundaries:

```typescript
'use client';

export default function Error({ error, reset }) {
  return (
    <div className="error-container">
      <h1>Something went wrong!</h1>
      <p>{error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={() => reset()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Loading States

Use Suspense for streaming:

```typescript
import { Suspense } from 'react';

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
        <DataComponent />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  );
}
```

These patterns make your Next.js applications faster and more maintainable!

# MongoDB Optimization: Indexing Strategies

Proper indexing can improve query performance by 10x or more. Here's what you need to know.

## Creating Basic Indexes

```javascript
db.users.createIndex({ email: 1 });
db.posts.createIndex({ createdAt: -1 });
db.products.createIndex({ sku: 1, warehouse: 1 });
```

## Compound Indexes (ESR Rule)

Follow the Equality, Sort, Range (ESR) rule:

```javascript
// For queries like: 
// db.orders.find({ status: 'shipped', customer: 'John' }).sort({ date: -1 })

db.orders.createIndex({
  status: 1,    // Equality
  customer: 1,  // Equality
  date: -1      // Sort
});
```

## Checking Index Usage

Analyze query execution:

```javascript
db.users.find({ email: 'test@example.com' }).explain('executionStats');
```

## Covering Queries

Return all data from the index:

```javascript
db.users.createIndex({
  email: 1,
  name: 1,
  status: 1
});

// This query doesn't need to fetch the document
db.users.find(
  { email: 'test@example.com' },
  { _id: 0, email: 1, name: 1, status: 1 }
);
```

## Index Best Practices

- Index fields used in WHERE clauses first
- Don't over-index (balance with write performance)
- Monitor unused indexes
- Remove duplicate indexes

Optimized queries mean happy users!

# React Performance Optimization Guide

React 18 brings powerful tools for performance. Let's master them.

## Memoization with memo()

```typescript
const UserCard = memo(({ user }) => {
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
});

const UserList = () => {
  const users = [{id: 1, name: 'John'}];
  return users.map(user => <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} />);
};
```

## useDeferredValue for Non-Urgent Updates

```typescript
import { useDeferredValue } from 'react';

export default function SearchResults({ searchInput }) {
  const deferredInput = useDeferredValue(searchInput);

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={searchInput} onChange={e => setInput(e.target.value)} />
      <SearchList query={deferredInput} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## useTransition for State Updates

```typescript
import { useTransition } from 'react';

export default function TabComponent() {
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
  const [tab, setTab] = useState('home');

  const handleTabChange = (newTab) => {
    startTransition(() => {
      setTab(newTab);
    });
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => handleTabChange('home')}>
        {isPending ? 'Loading...' : 'Home'}
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Code Splitting with Dynamic Imports

```typescript
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

const HeavyComponent = dynamic(
  () => import('@/components/HeavyComponent'),
  { loading: () => <p>Loading...</p> }
);

export default function Page() {
  return <HeavyComponent />;
}
```

These patterns ensure your React apps stay fast!
